WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.860
Welcome to Grade 6 English, Unit 8: Entertainment and Media.

2
00:00:04.340 --> 00:00:06.760
In today's world, entertainment is everywhere.

3
00:00:07.180 --> 00:00:11.320
We watch films and documentaries on
screens, listen to podcasts and radio shows,

4
00:00:11.700 --> 00:00:15.920
follow stories through books and graphic
novels, and even create our own content.

5
00:00:16.520 --> 00:00:19.100
But how much do we think about the media we consume?

6
00:00:19.760 --> 00:00:21.140
How does a film tell a story?

7
00:00:21.700 --> 00:00:23.720
What makes a documentary different from a drama?

8
00:00:24.400 --> 00:00:27.680
Why do some adverts stay in our
minds long after we have seen them?

9
00:00:28.580 --> 00:00:34.040
In this short lecture, preview the key
ideas and prepare for the independent lesson.

10
00:00:34.620 --> 00:00:41.160
By the end of this unit, you will be able to read and
understand texts about entertainment and media, including film,

11
00:00:41.420 --> 00:00:43.280
television, and storytelling traditions.

12
00:00:43.860 --> 00:00:46.740
You will also use participle adjectives correctly,

13
00:00:47.320 --> 00:00:53.160
choosing between the -ed form (how someone feels)
and the -ing form (what causes the feeling).

14
00:00:53.880 --> 00:01:00.620
You will also form sentences in the past simple
passive to describe events in film and history (e.g.

15
00:01:00.840 --> 00:01:02.880
"The documentary was filmed in Mogadishu").

16
00:01:03.220 --> 00:01:05.420
Keep these goals in mind as you work.

17
00:01:05.960 --> 00:01:11.920
Your vocabulary work begins with these words:
difference, enjoyment, excitement, grammatical, happiness,

18
00:01:12.280 --> 00:01:13.000
notwithstanding.

19
00:01:13.800 --> 00:01:18.700
Listen to each pronunciation in the
vocabulary lab, study its meaning in context,

20
00:01:19.260 --> 00:01:22.220
and use the word accurately in
your own discussion and writing.

21
00:01:22.940 --> 00:01:30.360
The reading sequence includes Why Entertainment Matters
and Screens, Stories, and the Art of Telling Tales.

22
00:01:31.160 --> 00:01:31.880
Read actively.

23
00:01:32.640 --> 00:01:37.080
Identify each text's central ideas,
notice viewpoint and reliability,

24
00:01:37.640 --> 00:01:40.920
and support every inference with
relevant evidence from the text.

25
00:01:41.500 --> 00:01:47.040
The first language focus is
Participle Adjectives (-ed and -ing).

26
00:01:47.980 --> 00:01:51.000
Participle adjectives come from verbs but describe nouns.

27
00:01:51.580 --> 00:01:57.480
The -ed form tells us how a person feels; the
-ing form tells us what causes the feeling.

28
00:01:58.220 --> 00:02:03.980
Notice the structure in the model examples, then apply
it deliberately in connected sentences and paragraphs.

29
00:02:04.900 --> 00:02:08.300
In speaking, you will complete
Speaking 1: Media Habits Interview.

30
00:02:08.670 --> 00:02:13.020
Organise your ideas, use evidence
and respond clearly to questions.

31
00:02:13.540 --> 00:02:16.340
In writing, you will plan, draft, check and revise.

32
00:02:17.120 --> 00:02:19.730
Use the success criteria before submitting your work.

33
00:02:19.980 --> 00:02:21.580
You are ready to begin.

34
00:02:22.180 --> 00:02:27.660
Start with the vocabulary lab, continue through
reading and comprehension, apply the grammar focus,

35
00:02:28.180 --> 00:02:30.480
and complete the speaking and writing practices.

36
00:02:31.300 --> 00:02:37.340
Use the AI tutor for hints and feedback any time, and
bring your questions to a live session too if you join one.
